(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-immunology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Blood & Immunology — Quiz 1 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Neutrophils are the body's first cellular responders to bacterial infection because they...","choices":["are numerous, circulate ready to act, and rapidly phagocytize bacteria","secrete antibodies that specifically neutralize the invading bacteria","mature slowly in the tissue before they can begin any phagocytosis","live for months in tissues, waiting to present antigen to lymphocytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are the most abundant circulating white cell and are already mature, so they marginate, undergo diapedesis, and phagocytize bacteria within about an hour of infection, making them the earliest cellular defense. — Blood & Immunology, slides 3-13, 28-30"},{"q":"Eosinophils are especially important in defending against...","choices":["intracellular viruses, which they lyse using secreted perforins","extracellular bacteria, which they clear mainly by opsonization","parasitic worms, which they attack by releasing toxic granule proteins","fungal spores, which they engulf through receptor-mediated endocytosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Eosinophils specialize against large parasites such as helminths; they attach to the worm and release toxic granule contents (including major basic protein) that damage it, since the parasite is too big to phagocytize. — Blood & Immunology, slide 33"},{"q":"Eosinophils are especially important in defense against...","choices":["producing the majority of the body's antibodies","clotting, by helping platelets form a plug during this particular process","oxygen transport to actively infected tissue","parasitic worm infections, and they also modulate allergic responses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Eosinophils specialize in attacking large parasites such as helminths, releasing toxic granule contents onto them, and they also participate in modulating allergic and inflammatory reactions, which is why their numbers rise in parasitic infections and allergies. — Blood & Immunology, slides 13-17, 34"},{"q":"The pus that can form at a site of bacterial infection consists largely of...","choices":["excess plasma proteins that have precipitated during this process","dead neutrophils, tissue debris, and destroyed bacteria","red blood cells that have leaked from the vessels","newly formed antibodies concentrated at the site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pus is an accumulation of neutrophils that have died after phagocytizing bacteria, together with liquefied tissue debris and destroyed microbes; its formation reflects the intense but self-limited action of these first-responder phagocytes. — Blood & Immunology, slides 26-31"},{"q":"Compared with neutrophils, macrophages are able to...","choices":["phagocytize many more particles and survive to keep functioning","divide rapidly within the blood to expand their numbers","carry oxygen to the site to support the inflammatory response","produce specific antibodies against the ingested organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages are larger, longer-lived, and far more powerful phagocytes than neutrophils; they can engulf many organisms and large debris and continue functioning, whereas neutrophils typically ingest a limited number and then die. — Blood & Immunology, slides 29-31"},{"q":"Among the circulating white blood cells, the most numerous under normal conditions are the...","choices":["basophils, which release histamine during allergy","monocytes, which mature into tissue macrophages","eosinophils, which target parasitic worms during this particular process","neutrophils, the first-responding phagocytes of acute infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are normally the most abundant circulating white cells and are the first phagocytes to arrive at sites of acute bacterial infection, engulfing and destroying invaders before dying and contributing to pus. — Blood & Immunology, slides 12-17, 26-30"},{"q":"Lymph nodes contribute to immunity mainly by...","choices":["storing mature neutrophils until an infection calls them out","filtering lymph and exposing trapped antigens to resident lymphocytes","secreting digestive enzymes that break down circulating pathogens","manufacturing red blood cells that carry antigen to the spleen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As lymph percolates through the node, macrophages lining the sinuses trap antigens and present them, while resident B- and T-lymphocytes are positioned to encounter and respond to those antigens, mounting an adaptive response. — Blood & Immunology, slides 22, 39"},{"q":"T-lymphocytes are so named because they mature in the...","choices":["thymus, where they are selected for function and self-tolerance","tibial marrow, a specialized region of long bones under these conditions","thyroid, under the direct control of thyroid hormone","tonsils, which serve as their primary maturation site"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. T-cells complete their maturation in the thymus, where they undergo selection that keeps useful, self-tolerant clones and deletes strongly self-reactive ones; the 'T' reflects this thymic education. — Blood & Immunology, slides 40-42"},{"q":"Kupffer cells lining the liver sinusoids illustrate which principle of the monocyte-macrophage system?","choices":["Circulating platelets become tissue phagocytes at sites of slow blood flow","Monocytes settle in tissue as fixed macrophages that filter passing blood","Neutrophils mature in the liver before entering the general circulation","Lymphocytes convert into macrophages once they reach the portal vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: fixed tissue macrophages such as Kupffer cells strip bacteria from portal blood, filtering the circulation. — Blood & Immunology, slides 20-23"},{"q":"One way the lymphatic system supports immune surveillance is by...","choices":["absorbing dietary glucose directly and delivering it straight to circulating lymphocytes","producing antibodies within the lymphatic vessels themselves before lymph returns to blood","pumping freshly oxygenated blood directly to the sites of active infection in the tissues","returning interstitial fluid and any captured antigens through nodes before reaching the blood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymphatics collect interstitial fluid (and antigens or microbes within it) and route it through lymph nodes for immune screening before returning it to the bloodstream, so pathogens are intercepted en route. — Blood & Immunology, slides 22, 39"},{"q":"Opsonization increases phagocytosis of a bacterium by...","choices":["attracting neutrophils along a chemical gradient toward the microbe","coating it with antibody and complement C3b that phagocytes recognize","fusing lysosomes with the phagosome to form a digestive vesicle","digesting its protective capsule so the cell surface becomes rough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: opsonins such as antibody and C3b bind the microbe and mark it for recognition and ingestion by phagocytes. — Blood & Immunology, slides 15-16"},{"q":"Cytotoxic (CD8) T-cells destroy virus-infected cells by...","choices":["releasing perforins and enzymes that trigger the target cell's death","engulfing the entire infected cell by phagocytosis","coating the infected cell with antibody so it can be removed much later","secreting histamine that lyses the infected cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cytotoxic T-cells recognize antigen presented on MHC I of an infected cell, then release perforins that form pores and enzymes that enter and induce the target cell's death, eliminating the reservoir of viral replication. — Blood & Immunology, slide 59"},{"q":"Dendritic cells are considered the most potent antigen-presenting cells because they...","choices":["form the pores that lyse virus-infected host cells directly","store iron recovered from red cells for later hemoglobin synthesis","secrete the antibodies that tag pathogens throughout the body for destruction","specialize in capturing antigen and displaying it to activate naive T-cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dendritic cells are distributed throughout tissues, and their principal job is to capture antigen and present it on MHC to naive T-cells, making them the most effective initiators of adaptive responses. — Blood & Immunology, slide 55"},{"q":"One direct action of the complement cascade is to...","choices":["present antigen fragments to helper T-cells","form membrane pores that lyse the targeted microbe","generate memory cells for a faster second response","synthesize antibodies specific to the pathogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beyond opsonizing microbes and promoting inflammation, the complement cascade assembles a membrane attack complex that punches pores in the target's membrane, causing lysis — a direct killing mechanism of this innate system. — Blood & Immunology, slide 53"},{"q":"The complement system aids defense in several ways, including...","choices":["opsonizing microbes, attracting phagocytes, and lysing target cells","storing iron released from broken-down red cells","carrying oxygen to the site of an infection under most conditions in the body","producing the specific antibodies against a pathogen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Complement enhances immunity in multiple ways: it opsonizes microbes to promote phagocytosis, its fragments attract phagocytes (chemotaxis) and drive inflammation, and its terminal components form a membrane attack complex that lyses target cells. — Blood & Immunology, slides 52-53"},{"q":"Why does loss of helper T-cells (as in advanced HIV) cripple the whole immune response?","choices":["Helper T-cells manufacture essentially all of the body's circulating complement proteins","Helper T-cells physically block viruses from entering any host cells in the body","Helper T-cells are the only cells able to directly phagocytize bacteria","Helper T-cells release cytokines that coordinate both antibody and cytotoxic responses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Helper (CD4) T-cells secrete cytokines/lymphokines that stimulate B-cell antibody production, cytotoxic T-cell activity, and macrophage function; without them, the coordinated adaptive response collapses, which is why CD4 loss is so devastating. — Blood & Immunology, slides 57-58"},{"q":"Helper T-cells (CD4 cells) are central to adaptive immunity because they...","choices":["directly engulf and digest invading bacteria under most conditions in the body","release cytokines that activate and coordinate B-cells and cytotoxic T-cells","carry oxygen to lymphoid tissues during infection","form the physical barrier that keeps microbes out"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Helper T-cells act as coordinators: after recognizing antigen presented on MHC, they secrete cytokines that stimulate B-cells to make antibody and help activate cytotoxic T-cells and macrophages, so their loss severely cripples the whole adaptive response. — Blood & Immunology, slides 55-59"},{"q":"Opsonization enhances phagocytosis by...","choices":["dissolving the microbe's cell wall before it is engulfed","forming memory cells for a faster response next time during this process in the tissues","coating a microbe with antibody or complement so phagocytes grip it more easily","attracting white cells toward the infection along a gradient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Opsonization tags a pathogen with antibody or complement fragments that phagocyte receptors recognize; this greatly increases the efficiency with which neutrophils and macrophages attach to and engulf the organism. — Blood & Immunology, slides 30, 53"},{"q":"Primary lymphoid organs differ from secondary lymphoid organs in that primary organs are where lymphocytes...","choices":["are destroyed, whereas secondary organs store them as it is usually described here","produce antibodies, whereas secondary organs make red cells","are formed and mature, whereas secondary organs are where they meet antigen","encounter antigen, whereas secondary organs form them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Primary lymphoid organs (bone marrow and thymus) are where lymphocytes originate and mature; secondary lymphoid organs (lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils) are where mature lymphocytes encounter antigen and mount responses. — Blood & Immunology, slides 22-24, 40-44"},{"q":"Which statement about antibody isotypes is correct?","choices":["IgG appears first, while IgM is the antibody of allergic reactions","IgM appears first in a response, while IgG is the most abundant in plasma","IgE is the most abundant plasma antibody and mainly drives agglutination","IgA is produced only after IgG and functions inside infected cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. IgM is the earliest antibody produced in a primary response (and is a large pentamer with many binding sites), whereas IgG makes up roughly three-quarters of plasma immunoglobulin and dominates the later and secondary responses. — Blood & Immunology, slide 51"},{"q":"Why is the secondary response to a repeated antigen faster and stronger than the first?","choices":["Complement proteins are consumed the first time and later regenerate larger","Plasma cells from the first exposure never die and keep secreting antibody","The innate system is permanently reprogrammed to recognize that antigen","Memory B- and T-cells from the first exposure persist and react quickly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: memory lymphocytes generated on first exposure remain and mount a rapid, amplified secondary response. — Blood & Immunology, slide 48"},{"q":"Vaccination with a killed or attenuated organism works by...","choices":["exposing the immune system to antigen so memory cells form without disease","supplying preformed antibodies that act for a few weeks","boosting the nonspecific innate barriers such as the skin and gastric acid","permanently deleting lymphocytes that react to the pathogen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A vaccine presents the antigens of a pathogen (as killed or attenuated forms) so the adaptive system mounts a response and generates memory cells, conferring active immunity and rapid protection on later exposure — without causing the disease itself. — Blood & Immunology, slide 63"},{"q":"Self-tolerance is maintained largely because lymphocyte clones that strongly react to self are...","choices":["stored in lymph nodes and activated only during infection","coated with antibody so that they cannot reach any host tissue","deleted by apoptosis during development in the thymus or marrow","converted into memory cells that ignore the self-antigen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Developing T-cells in the thymus and B-cells in the marrow that bind self-antigens with high affinity are induced to undergo apoptosis (clonal deletion), removing most self-reactive clones and preventing autoimmunity. — Blood & Immunology, slides 41, 61"},{"q":"In the sequence of an acute inflammatory response, which cells typically arrive first, followed by which?","choices":["neutrophils arrive first, followed later by monocytes that become macrophages","red blood cells arrive first to wall off the infection","lymphocytes arrive first, before any phagocytes appear under most normal conditions","macrophages arrive first, followed by neutrophils days later"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are the earliest cellular responders, arriving within about an hour; over the following hours to days, monocytes migrate in and mature into macrophages that continue phagocytosis and clean-up, giving the characteristic two-wave cellular response. — Blood & Immunology, slides 26-31"},{"q":"Compared with the primary response, the secondary antibody response is...","choices":["identical in speed and magnitude to the first","limited to IgM with no IgG produced at all","faster, larger, and dominated by higher-affinity IgG","slower and weaker because memory cells have died off"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because memory B- and T-cells persist from the first exposure, a repeat encounter produces a response that is quicker, of greater magnitude, and dominated by high-affinity IgG rather than the IgM that led the primary response; this is the basis of booster immunizations. — Blood & Immunology, slide 48"},{"q":"Which is an example of an innate (nonspecific) defense?","choices":["high-affinity IgG antibodies tailored to one pathogen","clonal selection of a lymphocyte matching one antigen","intact skin and acidic gastric secretions that block or kill microbes","memory B-cells produced after a specific vaccination during this process"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Innate defenses are present from birth and act nonspecifically; physical and chemical barriers such as intact skin, mucus, cilia, and acidic stomach secretions prevent entry or destroy many microbes without needing prior exposure. — Blood & Immunology, slides 18-20, 60"},{"q":"In an immediate allergic reaction, symptoms arise when an allergen...","choices":["neutralizes complement so inflammation cannot occur","cross-links IgE on mast cells, triggering release of histamine","stimulates the marrow to overproduce red blood cells","is engulfed by cytotoxic T-cells that then release perforin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In allergy, IgE bound to mast cells and basophils is cross-linked by the allergen, causing these cells to degranulate and release histamine and other mediators; the resulting vasodilation, increased permeability, and smooth-muscle effects produce allergic symptoms. — Blood & Immunology, slides 64-65"},{"q":"Receiving preformed antibodies (for example, across the placenta) provides...","choices":["active immunity that is delayed but long-lasting","innate immunity that never involves any antibodies","permanent immunity through the recipient's own memory cells","passive immunity that is immediate but temporary"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Preformed antibodies protect right away because no immune response has to develop, but they are gradually degraded and no memory cells are formed, so the protection is short-lived (weeks). — Blood & Immunology, slide 63"},{"q":"Which pairing correctly matches a defense with its immune category?","choices":["Antigen-specific plasma cells — innate; lysozyme in tears — acquired","Complement proteins — acquired; helper T-cell clones — innate immunity","Gastric acid and intact skin — innate; circulating antibodies — acquired","Memory B-cells — innate; natural killer cells — acquired immunity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: skin and gastric acid are nonspecific innate barriers, while antigen-specific antibodies are acquired immunity. — Blood & Immunology, slides 35-37"},{"q":"In an immediate (type I) allergic reaction, symptoms arise when...","choices":["antigen cross-links IgE bound to mast cells, triggering histamine release","cytotoxic cells lyse host cells that display the offending antigen","antibodies precipitate with soluble antigen and deposit in tissues","T-cells slowly infiltrate the skin over one to two days after contact"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: in immediate hypersensitivity, antigen cross-links IgE on mast cells/basophils, causing degranulation and histamine release. — Blood & Immunology, slides 64-65"}]};
